### Advanced Usage

For advanced usage, you can use the `requests` library to make HTTP requests and retrieve responses directly. The `requests` library provides a simple API for making HTTP requests, handling redirects and cookies, and encrypting data.

Here's an example of how to use the `requests` library to retrieve HTML content from a website:
```python
import requests

# Send a GET request to the website and retrieve the response
response = requests.get('https://www.example.com')

# Check if the request was successful (status code 200)
if response.status_code == 200:
    # Print the HTML content of the website
    print(response.text)
else:
    print(""Request failed"")
```
You can also use the `requests` library to retrieve JSON data from a website, by setting the `headers` parameter to `{'Accept': 'application/json'}`:
```python
import requests

# Send a GET request to the website and retrieve the response
response = requests.get('https://api.example.com/data', headers={'Accept': 'application/json'})

# Check if the request was successful (status code 200)
if response.status_code == 200:
    # Print the JSON data returned by the website
    print(response.json())
else:
    print(""Request failed"")
```
You can also use the `requests` library to send POST requests and retrieve responses from a website, by setting the `data` parameter to the data you want to send in the request body:
```python
import requests

# Send a POST request to the website and retrieve the response
response = requests.post('https://api.example.com/login', data={'username': 'user', 'password': 'pass'})

# Check if the request was successful (status code 200)
if response.status_code == 200:
    # Print the HTML content of the website
    print(response.text)
else:
    print(""Request failed"")
```
You can also use the `requests` library to send requests with authentication credentials, by setting the `auth` parameter to a tuple containing the username and password:
```python
import requests

# Send a POST request to the website and retrieve the response
response = requests.post('https://api.example.com/login', auth=('user', 'pass'))

# Check if the request was successful (status code 200)
if response.status_code == 200:
    # Print the HTML content of the website
    print(response.text)
else:
    print(""Request failed"")
```
You can also use the `requests` library to send requests with a custom header, by setting the `headers` parameter to a dictionary containing the custom headers:
```python
import requests

# Send a POST request to the website and retrieve the response
response = requests.post('https://api.example.com/login', headers={'X-Custom-Header': 'custom_value'})

# Check if the request was successful (status code 200)
if response.status_code == 200:
    # Print the HTML content of the website
    print(response.text)
else:
    print(""Request failed"")
```
You can also use the `requests` library to send requests with a custom cookie, by setting the `cookies` parameter to a dictionary containing the custom cookies:
```python
import requests

# Send a POST request to the website and retrieve the response
response = requests.post('https://api.example.com/login', cookies={'custom_cookie': 'custom_value'})

# Check if the request was successful (status code 200)
if response.status_code == 200:
    # Print the HTML content of the website
    print(response.text)
else:
    print(""Request failed"")
